WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.832
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.832 --> 00:00:07.540
DIONNA GLAZE: Hi.

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.690
Thanks for sharing your
lunch time with me.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:12.870
I'm going to tell you
about this cool stuff

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:16.870
that we're working on,
after I tell you why.

00:00:16.870 --> 00:00:20.160
So some potential customers
have shared with us

00:00:20.160 --> 00:00:22.980
that there are some barriers
for them moving their workloads

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:26.140
to a cloud instead of on Prem.

00:00:26.140 --> 00:00:30.090
And the biggest three
factors are regulation,

00:00:30.090 --> 00:00:35.000
trust in companies,
and compromise,

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.440
and what can happen
to the data that

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:41.110
is on other people's machines.

00:00:41.110 --> 00:00:45.940
I'm not going to talk too
much about regulation.

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:47.210
Here we go.

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.090
It's the other button.

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:55.040
But I will tell you how
we aim to solve problems

00:00:55.040 --> 00:01:00.260
with trust and compromise with
our open source framework.

00:01:00.260 --> 00:01:03.980
All right, so by trust, I mean
you are running your software

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:05.840
on other people's
machines, and they

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:08.660
can be running
arbitrary software

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.230
to manage those machines,
and they can, unfortunately,

00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:13.790
sometimes get compromised.

00:01:13.790 --> 00:01:17.780
Not saying that
happens at Google.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:20.120
Now, beyond just
the companies, there

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.280
can be individual
administrators who

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:25.950
have access to these computers
so that they can do their jobs.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:28.880
And they can be compromised
in one way or the other

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:34.270
through phishing, or
they're overtly malicious.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:36.030
Now, the compromised
component is,

00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:37.960
when you're running
on these computers,

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:42.450
they can be subject to problems
in, like, the Linux kernel.

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:47.840
Everybody running on Linux is
open to these same problems.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.810
There is also being able to
break out of the DMA jail

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:55.970
from rogue peripherals,
and to just go grab--

00:01:55.970 --> 00:01:57.900
go snooping memory.

00:01:57.900 --> 00:01:59.790
And that's not so great.

00:01:59.790 --> 00:02:04.670
So when data is
accessible on a machine,

00:02:04.670 --> 00:02:09.720
it is also subject
to a legal discovery.

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:15.480
So we consider that, also,
as part of compromise factors

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:20.360
for confidential data.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.590
So enclaves are two
things in the abstract.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:28.910
They are isolation for
your execution and data,

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:31.160
and they are attestation.

00:02:31.160 --> 00:02:34.980
I want to tell you a little
bit about what that means.

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:40.100
So isolation is that the
operating system, the BIOS,

00:02:40.100 --> 00:02:43.550
hardware other than the CPU,
is all outside of your trust

00:02:43.550 --> 00:02:44.510
boundary.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.560
It is not able to see
any of your memory

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.350
or tamper with your
execution at all.

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:58.670
In a typical process, the OS has
full control over your process.

00:02:58.670 --> 00:03:01.850
The BIOS can be tampered with.

00:03:01.850 --> 00:03:05.780
The hardware can have
access beyond what you want.

00:03:09.370 --> 00:03:14.190
Right, and when this data is
not even available to the kernel

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:16.440
and is only available
to your enclave,

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:21.720
it is not subject
to legal discovery.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:24.840
The second component of
an enclave is attestation.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:28.230
And attestation aims
to answer the question,

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:31.110
with cryptographic guarantees,
that the software you're

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:33.480
talking to is really the
software that you want

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:39.900
to be talking to, and that
it is running on the hardware

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.660
that you expect to be giving
you these Trusted Execution

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:47.010
Environment guarantees.

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:51.770
When you put these
two things together,

00:03:51.770 --> 00:03:55.600
you're able to make the
following statement, that you

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:59.080
send secrets only
to your software,

00:03:59.080 --> 00:04:01.940
and only your software
can read them.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:05.950
And that's really important to
a lot of potential customers

00:04:05.950 --> 00:04:09.360
who want to move to Cloud,
for a lot of reasons.

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:17.190
OK, so isolation, attestation
are nice properties

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:19.500
to say that an enclave
is in the abstract,

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:23.232
but unfortunately, that is
not an industry standard.

00:04:23.232 --> 00:04:24.690
And there are a
lot of technologies

00:04:24.690 --> 00:04:27.810
that market themselves as
enclaves or Trusted Execution

00:04:27.810 --> 00:04:32.340
Environments that don't give
all aspects of isolation

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.350
and attestation
that we really want.

00:04:35.350 --> 00:04:38.372
And because it's
too early to know

00:04:38.372 --> 00:04:40.080
which one of these
technologies is really

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.560
going to win in the
marketplace, it's

00:04:43.560 --> 00:04:47.250
nice to have your enclave
applications be written

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:50.670
for an abstract
notion of an enclave,

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:52.830
so that you can move around.

00:04:56.560 --> 00:04:57.760
And that's what Asylo is.

00:04:57.760 --> 00:04:58.260
Right?

00:04:58.260 --> 00:05:04.510
It is a framework that abstracts
over enclave technology

00:05:04.510 --> 00:05:09.720
and notions of attestation
so that you can easily

00:05:09.720 --> 00:05:15.120
migrate between different
Trusted Execution back ends.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:18.510
And now, a really,
really awesome cool thing

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:27.360
that is in Asylo is support
for a POSIX-based programming.

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:32.590
Now, when you're in one of these
Trusted Execution Environments,

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:38.970
you have that the OS is outside
of your trusted computing

00:05:38.970 --> 00:05:42.220
base, which means that
you don't get to use it,

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:45.750
which means it's really
hard to write software

00:05:45.750 --> 00:05:49.930
in the typical way that
people usually write software.

00:05:49.930 --> 00:05:55.590
And now to talk a bit more
about how the POSIX system works

00:05:55.590 --> 00:06:00.320
in Asylo, I want to introduce
my colleague, Chong Cai.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:02.820
CHONG CAI: I'm going to briefly
talk about the POSIX support

00:06:02.820 --> 00:06:04.350
that Asylo provides.

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:08.730
So, as Dionna talked
about earlier, when you're

00:06:08.730 --> 00:06:13.260
inside an enclave, it
provides security isolation

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:14.890
even from the operating system.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:18.390
So we're having trouble for
code running inside enclave

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.000
to access the resources
that's usually provided

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:22.600
by the operating system.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:28.350
So Asylo provides a POSIX layer
to make user code get access

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:29.280
to them.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:34.050
The way, so which includes
how to access clock,

00:06:34.050 --> 00:06:37.470
IO like readwrite, and
other kinds of syscalls

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:39.840
that you normally
want to access.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:43.260
We implement them with
security features added.

00:06:43.260 --> 00:06:46.980
So normally, when an
application makes a syscall,

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:49.980
it access the operating
system for the operation.

00:06:49.980 --> 00:06:52.090
But in enclave
case, code running

00:06:52.090 --> 00:06:54.850
is enclave is isolated
from the operating system.

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:56.310
So that's not the option.

00:06:56.310 --> 00:07:00.810
And we provide, Aslyo
provides a POSIX layer,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.600
which is an API level
of implementation

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:04.470
for the syscalls.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:07.590
So the user code that's
running inside an enclave

00:07:07.590 --> 00:07:09.528
does not need to
know, OK, this code

00:07:09.528 --> 00:07:11.070
is running inside
the enclave, I need

00:07:11.070 --> 00:07:13.320
to access the Asylo POSIX API.

00:07:13.320 --> 00:07:18.150
They can just access
exactly the syscall as user.

00:07:18.150 --> 00:07:26.020
And Asylo POSIX layer would run
that syscall and in some cases,

00:07:26.020 --> 00:07:28.900
it exits the enclave
and delegates

00:07:28.900 --> 00:07:31.920
the syscall to the operating
system with security features

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:33.960
added.

00:07:33.960 --> 00:07:35.880
The goal for Asylo
POSIX layer is

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:40.080
to provide exactly the same
behavior as if it's on a host.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.930
The user code does
not need to know it's

00:07:42.930 --> 00:07:44.250
running inside the enclave.

00:07:44.250 --> 00:07:47.490
They say they can call exactly
the same syscall as they would

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:50.100
on the host, and
they should expect

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:51.990
exactly the same behavior.

00:07:51.990 --> 00:07:54.640
The way we do that
is, for example,

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:59.070
for non-sensitive operations,
it may just exit the enclave

00:07:59.070 --> 00:08:01.050
and delegate the
call to the host.

00:08:01.050 --> 00:08:03.960
And there are some other
interesting examples

00:08:03.960 --> 00:08:07.170
that we provide to
achieve this goal.

00:08:07.170 --> 00:08:08.910
For example, signals.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:12.720
When somebody registers a
handler inside an enclave,

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:16.920
the Asylo POSIX layer saves
that handler inside the enclave,

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:22.050
then it exits the enclave,
register a handler on a host.

00:08:22.050 --> 00:08:25.050
When that signal arrives,
the handler on the host

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.300
is invoked, which
invokes an entry

00:08:27.300 --> 00:08:30.330
point to enter the
enclave and invoke

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:33.480
the corresponding handler that's
registering inside the enclave.

00:08:33.480 --> 00:08:36.030
So the user can expect
the same behavior

00:08:36.030 --> 00:08:38.322
they register handler
inside the enclave

00:08:38.322 --> 00:08:40.530
and when the signal arrives,
their handlers should be

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:41.530
[INAUDIBLE].

00:08:41.530 --> 00:08:44.550
Another interesting
example is a fork.

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:47.370
Fork is a syscall
that limits users

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:50.820
to create a process that's
exact the same as a parent.

00:08:50.820 --> 00:08:55.090
However, enclaves are not
clonable for security reason.

00:08:55.090 --> 00:08:58.170
So, in order to support
that, we provide

00:08:58.170 --> 00:09:01.980
duplication and encryption
of the enclave states.

00:09:01.980 --> 00:09:03.930
So we load a new enclave.

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.930
We encrypt the old
enclave data and restore

00:09:06.930 --> 00:09:10.800
that in the new enclave.

00:09:10.800 --> 00:09:14.770
The security features for fork
is not fully implemented yet.

00:09:14.770 --> 00:09:17.460
We're working actively
to support secure fork.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:21.300
And even though we are
providing security features

00:09:21.300 --> 00:09:23.370
for the syscalls
that we provide,

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:27.120
sometimes it's not impossible
to make it fully secure.

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:29.250
On our website
aslylo.dev, we have

00:09:29.250 --> 00:09:33.910
documentations fully detailed
of syscalls we provide.

00:09:33.910 --> 00:09:38.860
And if there is any possible
security concern or issues

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:44.717
it may have, the user code
should investigate into that

00:09:44.717 --> 00:09:45.800
before they make syscalls.

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:51.600
And so what do we have support
right now for Asylo POSIX

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:55.480
layer, is we have provide
enough POSIX layer support

00:09:55.480 --> 00:09:59.050
to run a fully featured GRPC.

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:03.290
We can also run a full feature
latest open source Redis.

00:10:03.290 --> 00:10:07.150
I will do a quick demo later
to show how we can do that.

00:10:07.150 --> 00:10:10.780
And in both cases, we don't
need to make any source change

00:10:10.780 --> 00:10:12.830
to either of them.

00:10:12.830 --> 00:10:16.060
We can just to pull exactly
the same code from the GitHub

00:10:16.060 --> 00:10:17.820
and run that inside
an enclave with Asylo.

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:24.400
So you're welcome to check
out our website aslylo.dev

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:29.560
if you're interested for updates
and documentations for Asylo.

00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:35.440
And our open source project
is at github.com/google/asylo.

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:37.470
Can check it out if
you're interested.

00:10:37.470 --> 00:10:41.500
And I'm going to do a quick
demo to show how you can

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:46.810
run Redis inside an enclave.

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:52.870
So what do you need to
run and use application

00:10:52.870 --> 00:10:56.390
like Redis inside an
enclave is a workspace.

00:10:56.390 --> 00:11:01.120
So first you need
a workspace file.

00:11:01.120 --> 00:11:04.510
Sorry, the color is probably
a little bit hard to read.

00:11:04.510 --> 00:11:09.312
So, right here, you
want to import--

00:11:09.312 --> 00:11:11.020
somehow it's not
showing the whole thing.

00:11:11.020 --> 00:11:13.620
But you want to import
Asylo right here.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:17.140
Asylo.3.4 is the latest
release that we have.

00:11:17.140 --> 00:11:18.880
And, of course, you
want to import Redis,

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:21.280
if that's the use application
you want to be build.

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:25.060
The latest open source,
which is Redis 5.0.4.

00:11:25.060 --> 00:11:30.970
Asylo provides a build
file in Asylo code base

00:11:30.970 --> 00:11:38.110
that builds Redis with Bazel,
which is the Google build tool.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:48.320
And, other than that, you
need a dot Bazel RC file.

00:11:48.320 --> 00:11:51.200
This basically tells
Bazel what tool chain,

00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:55.580
what cross tool to use, and
what the build configs are.

00:11:55.580 --> 00:11:59.308
This is exactly the same file
copied from Asylo GitHub.

00:11:59.308 --> 00:12:00.725
We don't need to
make any changes.

00:12:03.690 --> 00:12:07.370
Finally, the last thing
you need is a build target

00:12:07.370 --> 00:12:10.080
to tell Bazel what
do you want to build.

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:13.620
So here we provide an
application wrapper,

00:12:13.620 --> 00:12:15.970
which wraps the
user main function

00:12:15.970 --> 00:12:19.980
entrance in the trusted part
of the code, the raw method.

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:23.090
So the only thing
we needed is to wrap

00:12:23.090 --> 00:12:27.300
that in cc enclave binary, which
is our application wrapper.

00:12:27.300 --> 00:12:28.640
You give it a name.

00:12:28.640 --> 00:12:32.530
And you put what you want
to build as a build target

00:12:32.530 --> 00:12:34.650
in the dependencies.

00:12:34.650 --> 00:12:39.480
So here Redis main is a target
that runs the Redis server.

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:42.340
So that's everything you need.

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:48.480
Now, we can just build
application wrapper.

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:52.850
So Bazel is the
Google build tool.

00:12:52.850 --> 00:12:55.970
So we give it a config,
which is enc sim.

00:12:55.970 --> 00:13:00.500
Tells it to build the targets
in simulated environment.

00:13:00.500 --> 00:13:05.960
And Asylo Redis is the target
that we just add for cc enclave

00:13:05.960 --> 00:13:07.730
binary.

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:09.320
Here it is.

00:13:09.320 --> 00:13:11.170
You can see that it
builds very quickly.

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:13.220
It's speakers I
have configured it

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:15.890
because we don't have enough
time to build it right here.

00:13:15.890 --> 00:13:18.530
OK, so once you
have it built, we

00:13:18.530 --> 00:13:23.870
are ready to run the binary,
which is placed in the Bazel

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:27.890
bin, and this is the
binary we just built.

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:31.380
And we run a binary, we can see
that Redis server is running,

00:13:31.380 --> 00:13:32.150
is up.

00:13:32.150 --> 00:13:33.650
It's running inside the enclave.

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:36.200
Here we have a warning
about current maximum

00:13:36.200 --> 00:13:39.230
open file is 1,024.

00:13:39.230 --> 00:13:41.930
That's because this is
the current maximum file

00:13:41.930 --> 00:13:46.230
descriptors we allow with
Asylo inside an enclave.

00:13:46.230 --> 00:13:49.670
And so now it's ready
to accept connections.

00:13:49.670 --> 00:13:53.540
So in a different terminal,
we run Redis client

00:13:53.540 --> 00:13:55.640
to connect to the server.

00:13:55.640 --> 00:14:03.710
We can pin it and we can set
whatever key and a value.

00:14:03.710 --> 00:14:10.340
And I'll get it just to like
normally you would use Redis.

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:14.450
OK, that's everything
I have for the demo.

00:14:14.450 --> 00:14:15.800
Is there any questions?

00:14:15.800 --> 00:14:16.910
SPEAKER: Thank you, Chong.

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:18.640
CHONG CAI: Thank you.

